<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1807">1807</a>–<a href="/years/1882">1882</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Longfellow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry Wadsworth Longfellow was a nineteenth-century American poet, author of works such as The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiawatha">Hiawatha</a>. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire</a> corresponded with him. The site cites this link as an example of Pedro II's personal intellectual engagement with the wider world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-wadsworth-longfellow.jpg" alt="Henry Wadsworth Longfellow" width="304" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julia Margaret Cameron · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henry_Wadsworth_Longfellow,_photographed_by_Julia_Margaret_Cameron_in_1868.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pedro II studied languages including Hebrew, Sanskrit and Tupi, and corresponded with Louis Pasteur and <b>Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brazilian-empire">Brazilian Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</b> was its first American translator, and it has been translated into English more times than into any other language, and poets including Seamus Heaney, Robert Pinsky and W. S. Merwin have translated all or parts of it.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The song itself has been translated into several languages, among them an English version published by the American poet <b>Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/malbrough-sen-va-t-en-guerre">Malbrough s'en va-t-en guerre</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry Wadsworth Longfellow</b> and Robert Bridges made further translations.</blockquote><a href="/songs/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron (O Gladsome Light)</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Portland, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Cambridge, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.66,-70.26&amp;year=1855" aria-label="Portland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2862 616 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry Wadsworth Longfellow"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3312 886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3312 886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.66,-70.26&amp;year=1855">What was here in 1855? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Lives of great men all remind us We can make our lives sublime, And, departing, leave behind us Footprints on the sands of time…&#8221;</p><footer>A Psalm of Life (1838) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1365/pg1365-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Thou, too, sail on, O Ship of State! Sail on, O UNION, strong and great! Humanity with all its fears, With all the hopes of future years, Is hanging breathless on thy fate!&#8221;</p><footer>The Building of the Ship (1849) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1365/pg1365-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Ships that pass in the night, and speak each other in passing, Only a signal shown and a distant voice in the darkness; So on the ocean of life we pass and speak one another…&#8221;</p><footer>Tales of a Wayside Inn, part 3 (1873), the Theologian's tale &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1365/pg1365-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Toiling,—rejoicing,—sorrowing, Onward through life he goes; Each morning sees some task begin, Each evening sees it close Something attempted, something done, Has earned a night's repose.&#8221;</p><footer>The Village Blacksmith (1840) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1365/pg1365-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Under a spreading chestnut-tree The village smithy stands; The smith, a mighty man is he, With large and sinewy hands; And the muscles of his brawny arms Are strong as iron bands.&#8221;</p><footer>The Village Blacksmith (1840) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/1365/pg1365-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
